// An append-only log, as the vocabulary's `sync` kind at `type: log`.
//
// # What it is, and what it is not
//
// Records go on the end and nothing rewrites what is already there. A reader
// reads FORWARD from a position it keeps itself, and reading consumes nothing —
// which is the whole difference from a queue. A queue hands each item to
// exactly one consumer and the item is gone once acknowledged; a log keeps
// every record and serves it to every reader that asks. Work distribution
// versus history.
//
// It is not a `stream` either: a stream is fan-out with no retention, so a
// subscriber that was not attached missed it. A log is durable and replayable
// from any position it still holds.
//
// # `append` and `read`, and no third verb
//
// THE ABSENCE OF `put` AND `delete` IS WHERE APPEND-ONLY IS STATED. A record
// that can be overwritten or removed is not one, and a verb set is the one
// place this vocabulary says so where every consumer reads it back.
//
// A FOLLOW is the other direction — the platform handing each new record over
// as it lands — and it is deliberately absent. It is a delivery, the way a
// queue's `receive` is, so it needs a dispatcher rather than a function a guest
// calls. `read` is a pull, and it is what a log owes first.
//
// # The position is an opaque cursor and not an offset
//
// Every log client in the world hands you a dense integer offset, and this one
// deliberately does not, because the vocabulary lets an author choose what
// happens to two concurrent appends:
//
//   - `consensus: raft` — one writer decides, so there IS one total
//     order and a dense position would be meaningful.
//   - `consensus: crdt` — two nodes append at the same instant and neither can
//     assign "the next one" without asking the other, which is the coordination
//     a crdt exists to avoid. The order CONVERGES rather than being decided.
//
// A dense offset is a promise only the first of those can keep. An opaque
// cursor is one shape both keep, so a guest written today against a raft log
// still compiles, still reads forward, and still resumes where it stopped when
// the log it names is a crdt one. An offset now would buy nothing and cost a
// second, incompatible read verb later.
//
// A cursor is only ever handed back to the log that issued it. Nothing about
// its contents is promised, and comparing two of them is not meaningful.
package taubyte:log@0.1.0;

interface records {
    // One record, as it was appended.
    record entry {
        // Where this record sits. Hand it back as `after` to read what follows.
        cursor: string,

        // Unix seconds when the log accepted it. It is the LOG's clock and not
        // the appender's, because what a log orders is when it took the record,
        // and a caller's clock is a number a caller chose.
        at: s64,

        data: list<u8>,
    }

    variant error {
        // The label is not in this resource's `sync:` block.
        no-such-log(string),
        // Declared, but not with this op — or the target is a `sync` this cloud
        // cannot serve as a log, which an author can read.
        denied(string),
        // The node that took the call is not the one that may write. Under
        // `raft` exactly one node appends at a time, and this is its own arm
        // because a caller RETRIES it and nothing else about the request was
        // wrong.
        not-writable(string),
        // One record larger than this log will take.
        too-large(string),
        // A cursor this log never issued, or one whose records retention has
        // already dropped. It is its own arm because a caller FIXES it — by
        // starting again from the beginning — rather than retrying it.
        bad-cursor(string),
        unavailable(string),
    }

    // append adds one record to the end and answers the cursor it landed at, so
    // a caller can record what it wrote without reading it back.
    append: func(name: string, data: list<u8>) -> result<string, error>;

    // read answers the records after a cursor, in order, oldest first. An empty
    // `after` starts at the oldest record the log still holds.
    //
    // FEWER THAN `limit` MEANS CAUGHT UP, which is how a reader knows to stop
    // without a second call that answers nothing. `limit` of 0 asks for this
    // cloud's own ceiling, which is also the cap on anything larger.
    read: func(name: string, after: string, limit: u32) -> result<list<entry>, error>;
}

world imports {
    import records;
}
